      "stageRecord": "A past-tense T merges with each v domain. Each previously formed V-v complex then raises to T, leaving a silent lower copy of that complex. The pronounced verbs consequently precede the subjects, which remain in their original v-specifier positions. This analysis does not impose an English-style requirement that finite T must attract the subject. T agrees with the accessible external argument and licenses its nominative Case; transitive v licenses the pronominal object's accusative Case. Relative-clause agreement is masculine singular with سامر, whereas main-clause agreement is feminine singular with ليلى. The feminine inflection already present in قرأت realizes the latter agreement. The object clitics remain syntactically distinct from the raised verbal heads, with their surface associations updated to the highest pronounced verb occurrences.",
      "statement": "Construct the finite clauses and derive their verb-before-subject order.",
